This was the result for array sizes varying from 1KB to 1GB:
# size_bytes  size_KB  ns_consecutive  ns_random
        1024         1       1.435     1.435
        2048         2       1.435     1.435
        4096         4       1.374     1.367
        8192         8       1.357     1.357
       16384        16       1.357     1.357
       32768        32       1.361     1.363
       65536        64       1.358     2.620
      131072       128       1.358     3.221
      262144       256       1.358     3.513
      524288       512       1.358     4.885
     1048576      1024       1.359     7.274
     2097152      2048       1.360    14.043
     4194304      4096       1.360    18.025
     8388608      8192       1.360    19.552
    16777216     16384       1.362    24.866
    33554432     32768       1.384    43.297
    67108864     65536       1.401    69.807
   134217728    131072       1.396    84.361
   268435456    262144       1.406    95.078
   536870912    524288       1.407     103.511
  1073741824   1048576       1.407     108.857

Being the frequency of the processor and the sizes of the cache
Cpu baseMHz:           2300.0000
CPU max MHz:           3700.0000
CPU min MHz:           1000.0000
L1d cache:             32K
L1i cache:             32K
L2 cache:              1024K
L3 cache:              25344K

We can see how for the consecutive pointers in memory the time per acces is
around 5 clock cycles:
   if frequency = 3700 * 10**6 Hz
   5 * frequency * 10**9 ns = 1.35 ns
Even if the size of the array goes beyond the cache. This due to the prefetcher
that correctly deduce your next memory call and bring it to cache before you ask for it.

The non consecutive pointers case behave as expected. Each call brings a new cache
line for the respective L2,L3 or RAM if necessary, thus measuring latency.

The reason why the 1KB and 2KB arrays lasts more per acces could be because the CPU
had not yet increase the frequency to its maximum, then after some iterations the cpu
release its full power and then the results are the predicted ones.


